.TH SET_ITERATOR_FIND 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
set_iterator_find \- Find an element in a set using an iterator

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/set.h>
.IB #include <lib/byte.h>

.IB int
.IB set_iterator_find(set_iterator_t* it, const void* x, size_t n);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBset_iterator_find\fR function finds an element in a set using an iterator. It takes three arguments: the iterator, the element to find, and the size of the element. The function returns 1 if the element is found, and 0 if the iterator is invalid or the element is not found.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB it
The iterator to use to find the element.
.TP
.IB x
The element to find.
.TP
.IB n
The size of the element.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The function returns 1 if the element is found, and 0 if the iterator is invalid or the element is not found.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBset_iterator_find\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/set.h>
#include <lib/byte.h>

int main() {
    set_t set;
    set_iterator_t it;
    set_init(&set);

    set_inserts(&set, "apple");
    set_inserts(&set, "banana");
    set_inserts(&set, "cherry");

    const char* search = "banana";
    size_t search_size = strlen(search);

    if(set_iterator_find(&it, search, search_size)) {
        printf("Found element: %.*s\n", (int)search_size, search);
    } else {
        printf("Element not found\n");
    }

    set_free(&set);
    return 0;
}
.nef